before:
```swift
struct LabelSnapshot: Sendable { let text: String }
actor LabelStore {
    private var text = "Ready"
    func snapshot() -> LabelSnapshot { LabelSnapshot(text: text) }
}
```
String is Sendable. The returned struct contains a value, not direct access to the actor's stored property.

In a compatible top-level async program, follow the definitions with:
```swift
let store = LabelStore()
let saved = await store.snapshot()
print(saved.text)
```
This prints Ready. snapshot takes no explicit arguments and returns a LabelSnapshot value through the awaited call. Reading saved.text does not directly read actor storage. The exercise’s separate before/after values extend this same snapshot contract.

after:
```swift
struct LabelSnapshot: Sendable { let text: String }
actor LabelStore {
    private var text = "Ready"
    func snapshot() -> LabelSnapshot { LabelSnapshot(text: text) }
}
let store = LabelStore()
let saved = await store.snapshot()
print(saved.text)
```
In a Swift program that allows top-level await, this prints Ready. saved contains its own text value.
